/* Copyright 2026 Rhett Creighton - Apache License 2.0
 *
 * THE REMOTE CLASS TABLE — for every command leaf this binary registers, the
 * one decision "may a peer on our own mesh ask this node to run it?"
 *
 * Consumer must define:
 *   REMOTE_COMMAND_CLASS(leaf, class, reason)
 *
 * `leaf`    the canonical invocation name, exactly as registered:
 *           a dotted path from config/commands/<branch>.def (the typed
 *           registry expanded by config/src/command_catalog.c), or a flat
 *           method name from
 *           app/controllers/include/controllers/agent_contracts.def (the
 *           first-call agent API table).
 * `class`   exactly one of:
 *             REMOTE_CLASS_NEVER            refuse regardless of capability
 *             REMOTE_CLASS_OWNER_CAPABILITY require a live owner-minted
 *                                           capability that NAMES this leaf
 *             REMOTE_CLASS_READ_ONLY        bounded, side-effect-free read,
 *                                           reachable by any live owner-minted
 *                                           capability for this node
 * `reason`  one line saying why the leaf is remotable. REQUIRED for
 *           REMOTE_CLASS_READ_ONLY and REMOTE_CLASS_OWNER_CAPABILITY; empty
 *           for REMOTE_CLASS_NEVER, whose justification is the section comment
 *           above its group. Refusing needs no defence; permitting does.
 *
 * ── WHAT THIS FILE IS AND IS NOT ────────────────────────────────────────────
 * It is a CLASSIFICATION, not a switch. No transport reads it today, no
 * dispatcher consults it, and nothing here enables remote execution of
 * anything. The design it belongs to is docs/work/REMOTE_COMMAND_CHANNEL.md,
 * whose first increment is deliberately this table plus its gate. Landing the
 * decision before the mechanism is the point: when the transport is written it
 * inherits a table somebody already argued over, instead of an allowlist grown
 * one hurried leaf at a time next to the code that needed it.
 *
 * ── DEFAULT DENY ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * REMOTE_CLASS_NEVER is the default and the majority. A leaf with no row here
 * is not "unclassified, treat as read-only"; it is a lint failure
 * (tools/lint/check_remote_command_classes.sh), because an unclassified leaf is
 * a decision nobody made. The gate is symmetric: a row naming a leaf the
 * registry no longer has also fails, so a renamed leaf cannot leave a stale
 * permission behind under its old name.
 *
 * ── THE CLASS ONLY EVER SUBTRACTS ───────────────────────────────────────────
 * A class is a filter in front of the existing leaf, never a substitute for it.
 * Every local check a leaf already performs — ZCL_COMMAND_AUTH_OWNER,
 * hotswap_activation_authorized(), the wallet grant default-deny, the datadir
 * refusals — still runs afterwards, unchanged, on the target node. Nothing in
 * this file can grant an authority the leaf would refuse locally. That is why
 * REMOTE_CLASS_OWNER_CAPABILITY on a leaf that is already owner-gated locally
 * is not a widening: it adds a second, independent, revocable, expiring gate in
 * front of the first.
 *
 * ── HOW A CLASS WAS CHOSEN ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * READ_ONLY answers one question only: "what is this machine doing right now?"
 * Node, chain, sync, network, storage and runtime posture; registry discovery;
 * deterministic local projections. A leaf is NOT read_only merely because its
 * macro is a *_READ shape. Custody reads (balances, addresses, notes),
 * authority reads (which grants exist, which peers are paired, this node's
 * identity), general introspection primitives (arbitrary SQL, arbitrary state
 * dumps, arbitrary file reads), work-starting reads (benchmarks, self-tests,
 * waits) and economic reads are all NEVER.
 *
 * OWNER_CAPABILITY is deliberately tiny — seven leaves. Each is a thing the
 * owner concretely needs to do to a machine they own and cannot do any other
 * way, and each carries its own argument below.
 *
 * NEVER is everything else, including every leaf that spends, signs, exports
 * key material, mutates a datadir, changes who this node trusts, evaluates or
 * mutates a consensus predicate, publishes on the operator's behalf, or
 * executes fetched code.
 *
 * ── ORDER AND GROUPING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Promoted leaves come first, individually argued. The NEVER rows follow,
 * grouped by branch in registry order, with one section comment per branch
 * carrying that branch's argument. Rows are data; the reasoning lives beside
 * them on purpose. A list of names nobody can defend is a list the next lane
 * deletes.
 */

/* ── read_only ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
