/* OPS — 36 never_remote leaves.
 *
 * What survives the promotions above, by kind.
 *
 *   General introspection levers. ops.state dumps whatever a named subsystem
 *   chooses to emit, and ops.debug.producer / .profile / .backtrace / .rom are
 *   the same shape. The CATALOG of dumpable subsystems (ops.statecatalog) is a
 *   list of names and is read_only; the dump itself is not, because its content
 *   is decided by code this table cannot see.
 *
 *   Work starters. ops.selftest and ops.recovery.rebuild spend the target's
 *   resources on request.
 *
 *   ops.mesh.machines belongs in that group and reads like it does not, which
 *   is why it is called out rather than left in the list. Its name and its
 *   *_READ macro say "list paired machines", and its registered description
 *   says the rest: the call runs a bounded live refresh, probing up to 8 active
 *   records over the established Noise status lane on a collective 12-second
 *   budget and PERSISTING each verified receipt before rendering. It never
 *   dials, so it is not a reachability lever — but it is still one remote
 *   request that spends the target's time, touches eight other machines and
 *   writes durable state, which is not a side-effect-free read whatever the
 *   macro shape claims. It is also the pairing roster, and this table's own
 *   rule puts "which peers are paired" in NEVER. The read a fleet driver
 *   actually wants from one box is ops.mesh.status, promoted above, which
 *   projects reachability and dials nothing.
 *
 *   The trust surface this channel rides on. ops.mesh.identity reveals this
 *   node's identity; ops.mesh.pair.plan / .commit / .list / .revoke and
 *   ops.mesh.join change or enumerate who it trusts. Letting a remote caller
 *   edit the pairing that authorizes remote callers is a bootstrap loop with no
 *   floor.
 *
 *   The authority inventory. ops.telemetry.agents.sessions / .grants /
 *   .activity enumerate the capability grants themselves — the map an attacker
 *   would want before choosing a target.
 *
 *   Custody and economics telemetry. ops.telemetry.wallet.* is custody;
 *   ops.telemetry.metaverse.* and ops.telemetry.zcode.* are P4 economics, not
 *   "what is this machine doing".
 *
 *   Mutation and long-lived state. ops.config.reload and
 *   ops.debug.rom_seed.enable / .disable / .publish and
 *   ops.debug.rom_fetch.bundle change node configuration or publish on the
 *   node's behalf; ops.telemetry.watch opens a subscription rather than
 *   answering once. ops.config.show is refused with them: node configuration
 *   quotes datadir paths and operator layout.
 *
 *   Not yet real. ops.jobs.list, ops.postmortem.replay,
 *   ops.telemetry.alerts.history and ops.telemetry.storage.cache are PLANNED
 *   leaves that fail closed today. Classifying a PLANNED leaf by what its name
 *   suggests it will do is how a permission gets granted to code nobody has
 *   read; they stay NEVER until they exist and someone reclassifies them. */
REMOTE_COMMAND_CLASS("ops.jobs.list", REMOTE_CLASS_NEVER, "")
REMOTE_COMMAND_CLASS("ops.state", REMOTE_CLASS_NEVER, "")
REMOTE_COMMAND_CLASS("ops.selftest", REMOTE_CLASS_NEVER, "")
